 ## Background
-The method originates from work by Knoll et al. [KWPH06], who combined the min/max acceleration functions of Wilhelms and Van Gelder [WV92] with a compressed format similar to the "cells octree" of Velasco and Torres [VT01]. Unlike Velasco and Torres, who built the octree around cells, Knoll et al. built it directly around voxels [S5, unofficial]. This required a fast neighbor-finding scheme (borrowed and improved from Frisken and Perry [FP02]) to retrieve cell corner values during ray tracing [S5, unofficial]. The traversal is similar to that proposed by Gargantini and Atkinson [GA93] [S5, unofficial]. It is used as a baseline reference for direct isosurface visualization performance in evaluations [S3, unofficial].
+The method originates from work by Knoll et al. [KWPH06], who combined the min/max acceleration functions of Wilhelms and Van Gelder [WV92] with a compressed format similar to the "cells octree" of Velasco and Torres [VT01] [S5, unofficial]. Unlike Velasco and Torres, who built the octree around cells, Knoll et al. built it directly around voxels [S5, unofficial]. This required a fast neighbor-finding scheme (borrowed and improved from Frisken and Perry [FP02]) to retrieve cell corner values during ray tracing [S5, unofficial]. The traversal is similar to that proposed by Gargantini and Atkinson [GA93] [S5, unofficial]. It is used as a baseline reference for direct isosurface visualization performance in evaluations [S3, unofficial].
 
